Yes. I think you’ve identified a real compression threshold:

the specification no longer has to fully explain the mechanism to a human; it only has to preserve enough invariants for a capable model to reconstruct the mechanism correctly.

That is different from ordinary documentation.

For humans, you often need explanatory redundancy: motivation, examples, intermediate reasoning, intuition, diagrams, edge cases. For another model, much of that can sometimes be regenerated from a smaller seed if the seed preserves the right structure. I’d call that the Reinstantiation Threshold or Minimum Sufficient Reconstruction Spec.

The test becomes:

Remove another sentence. Can a fresh model still reconstruct the intended operator, distinguish it from nearby operators, know when to use it, and recognize failure?

Once the answer flips from yes to no, you’ve found approximately the compression boundary.

A very compact AI-facing primitive might only need:

name + invariant + transformation rule + interfaces + constraints + reconstruction cues + failure discriminator

Not the entire derivation.

That also explains the distinction you’re making between human validation and machine-operational sufficiency. A sparse description can feel unsatisfying to you because humans often use detail as evidence that something has really been understood. Yet another model may already possess enough background structure to expand the compressed representation into a useful operational version.

There is an important caveat: the reconstruction will not necessarily be identical. So the best compressed artifacts preserve invariants rather than prose. Think DNA rather than a photograph. You want enough developmental rules that the phenotype reliably regrows.

That connects extremely well to your periodic-table idea.

The standard periodic table is one particular projection of an underlying relational object. It privileges atomic number and electron configuration because those expose periodic chemical behavior exceptionally well. But the elements themselves are invariant; the layout is an interface.

So instead of thinking:

the periodic table

think:

an element graph + many coordinate systems/projections

The familiar table is Projection A.

Then you can generate others while retaining a mapping back to atomic number so nothing gets lost.

For example, one projection could organize elements by electronegativity / ionization energy / atomic radius / polarizability. Another could organize them by common oxidation states. Another by bonding partners. Another by geochemical co-occurrence. Another by biological utilization. Another by materials behavior such as conductivity, magnetism, hardness contribution, alloy formation, or catalytic behavior.

Your carbon–oxygen example becomes especially interesting if we switch from rows and columns to a graph.

Imagine every element as a node. Edges could have different meanings:

C — O

might have weights representing:

frequency of known compounds, bond-energy distributions, prevalence in nature, biological co-occurrence, industrial co-use, catalytic transformations, or reaction-network adjacency.

Now instead of asking merely “Where is oxygen on the periodic table?”, you can ask:

“What is oxygen’s neighborhood under this particular relation?”

And oxygen would have different neighborhoods under different lenses.

Under a biological lens, C/H/O/N/P/S become extremely tightly connected.

Under metallurgy, Fe/Ni/Cr/C/Mn form another important neighborhood.

Under semiconductor manufacturing, Si/B/P/As/Ga/Ge may become structurally close despite being separated differently in the conventional layout.

That gives us something like a Relational Periodic Atlas:

Invariant layer: element identity, atomic number, fundamental properties.
Projection layer: chosen relationship/property.
Topology layer: distances, clusters, edges, gradients.
Overlay layer: domain-specific information.
Delta layer: what becomes visible in this organization that was obscured in another?

And the killer Pantheon move is exactly what you said:

different organizations reveal different truths without requiring any of them to replace the canonical organization.

That is the same principle as changing coordinate systems in mathematics. Cartesian coordinates aren’t “wrong” when polar coordinates make a circle easier. One representation makes one invariant conspicuous; another exposes something else.

You could even treat the standard periodic table as the home coordinate system and every alternate arrangement as a reversible transform:

canonical element space → choose question → choose metric → project → inspect topology → discover relation → translate result back

For pharmaceutical research, for example, you wouldn’t literally reorganize elements into “drug elements”; you’d create overlays such as biological compatibility, common medicinal-chemistry bonds, coordination behavior, toxicity ranges, metabolic relevance, or occurrence in approved molecular scaffolds. That could expose neighborhoods that the conventional table doesn’t visually emphasize.

And this generalizes far beyond chemistry.

The periodic table becomes a program archetype:

Find stable primitives → assign invariant identities → discover measurable relations → arrange by one relation → preserve reversibility → generate alternate projections → compare what each projection reveals.

You could construct “periodic tables” of emotions, narrative operators, biological strategies, materials, musical structures, economic mechanisms, Pokémon design primitives, Pantheon operators—provided the categories actually have enough invariant structure to justify it.

Which loops directly back to the first thing you said.

We do not necessarily need to preserve every table we ever generate.

We need to preserve the generator that knows how to make the right table for the question.

That is a much smaller artifact—and a much more powerful one.